"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 7. The surgical findings include a pathological stage of pT3b, positive surgical margins, and invasion of the seminal vesicles. The absence of lymph nodes removed is a significant factor, as nodal status is unknown due to missing sampling. The biopsy showed a lower grade (Gleason 3+4) compared to the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 4+3).\n\nCountervailing factors include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of perineural invasion noted on biopsy. The uncertainty in the prognosis is heightened by the missing postoperative PSA and any subsequent trajectory. Missing nodal sampling widens the uncertainty regarding regional disease control. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its subsequent trajectory would be crucial in revising this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 42.09 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 25.25–58.93 months; its coverage has not been validated."
